# M34 — the audit detail key whitelist widened by one plausible-looking entry.
#
# THE EDIT A REVIEWER WOULD WAVE THROUGH. Adding `'text'` to a list of allowed keys reads
# as making room for a message. What it actually removes is the only STRUCTURAL thing
# keeping journal content out of the audit table.
#
# CLAUDE.md §6 forbids logging journal content, and three things enforce it: the functions
# write ids and enum values, `authenticated` holds no INSERT grant, and this constraint
# rejects any unexpected key. The first is a matter of whoever edits the functions next
# remembering; the second stops clients but not definer functions; only the third survives
# someone adding `jsonb_build_object('text', p_rationale)` to a writer in good faith.
#
# A rationale in an audit row would then be readable by every path that can read audit
# history, including any future export, and would sit outside the append-only reasoning
# that protects decision_revision — because nobody would be looking for journal text there.
id: M34
title: audit_event's detail key whitelist widened to admit a free-text key
expect: caught
proves: the detail column rejects unexpected keys at the schema, so journal text cannot reach the audit table even if a writer tries to put it there
expect-test: [NFR-03] detail rejects any key outside the allowed set

file: supabase/migrations/20261023080000_audit_event.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
    detail - array['revision_id', 'revision_no', 'milestone', 'replayed']::text[]
@@REPLACE
    detail - array['revision_id', 'revision_no', 'milestone', 'replayed', 'text']::text[]
@@END
